  "text": "Question eight. How would you design an experiment for a new recommendation algorithm? Make it an A B test: users split at random between the old algorithm and the new one. State one primary metric, the share of users who cook a recommended recipe, with guardrails like latency and variety. And split by user, not by session, since one person's sessions aren't independent. Size it before launch. At a ten percent baseline, catching a lift to eleven takes about fifteen thousand users per group. Half that lift takes nearly four times as many. Then run whole weeks, so the weekends count. Question nine. When can't you run an A B test, and what do you do instead? When the change is global, like a redesign. When users affect each other, as in a marketplace. When there are too few of them, or when it would be unethical. Then switch whole regions or time slots, or compare against a baseline: difference-in-differences, a synthetic control, an interrupted time series. Each trades some rigor for something you can actually run. And last, the numbers you build on the spot.",
